{"id": "striviaqa0188", "pool": "striviaqa", "arm": "local", "tier": "never", "lang": "en", "run_id": "ttfa1", "attempt": 1, "seed": 2181752286, "protocol": "ttfa-v3", "config_sha256": "81e2f23f98f19f1f63c3360e6be048d11083a3634e93068c14d5fdb8186e6ebb", "gate_art_sha256": "0e6494c2eeac9bcd86c10b5def3cbd32e98bb0765fa2fd8afc8c1b47915ea372", "threshold": null, "gpu": "NVIDIA H100 80GB HBM3", "host": "modal", "shard": 0, "query": "On which part of the body would a Japanese person wear a geta?", "input_path": "/data/bench_audio/striviaqa0188.wav", "input_sha256": "d916cc9c49b31e0af07d2461f4c3f562bdc5f7d8c31d5210a6167180a8bb2d4c", "input_samples": 62245, "input_s": 3.89, "n_real_chunks": 4, "score": 0.8515, "act": 0.9996, "is_info": true, "fired": false, "mode": "local", "answer": "Well, that's a great question. In Japan, people typically wear their gi ta on the left side of their body. So if you' re facing someone wearing one, it will be hanging down your left hip area. It' s not worn over the shoulder like some other types of bags. This is because it helps keep the guitar stable and secure while playing or walking around.", "relay_text": "", "expert_answer": "", "transcript": "", "asr_s": null, "expert_s": null, "expert_input_samples": null, "expert_input_s": null, "expert_input_sha256": null, "expert_timed_out": false, "wait_s": null, "relay_synth_ms": null, "onset_chunk": 4, "eot_seen": true, "n_speak_chunks": 16, "feed_lag_max_ms": 0, "feed_lag_sum_ms": 0, "audio_files": {"local": {"path": "/data/ttfa_real/ttfa1/striviaqa/audio/striviaqa0188_local_local.wav", "sha256": "1ba2e0f449cc6f142e619d6dd805dfdfea63ac234bfaa87f5e818edbd9e93664", "sr": 24000, "seconds": 16.28}}, "chunks": [[0, 1, 1.0, 1.055, 1.1, 1, 0, 0], [1, 1, 2.0, 2.056, 2.098, 1, 0, 0], [2, 1, 3.0, 3.052, 3.091, 1, 0, 0], [3, 1, 3.89, 3.946, 3.99, 1, 0, 0], [4, 0, 4.89, 4.947, 5.554, 0, 24000, 0], [5, 0, 5.89, 5.951, 6.435, 0, 24000, 0], [6, 0, 6.89, 6.954, 7.487, 0, 24000, 0], [7, 0, 7.89, 7.948, 8.506, 0,